      ],
      "text": "Situational analysis must precede training because a request for training is merely a reported concern, not a verified diagnosis. A visible performance problem does not establish its underlying cause; for example, weaknesses may stem from supervision, equipment, or procedures rather than individual skills. By conducting analysis first, advisers can investigate three levels of capacity: the enabling environment (legal and political context), the institution or unit (systems and logistics), and the practitioner (individual competencies). This diagnostic path allows for verifying evidence to ensure the selected response, such as training or structural changes, genuinely addresses the identified capacity gap within the five areas of police services, enabling services, policy formulation, accountability, and stakeholder engagement.\n\nINVENTED TEACHING EXAMPLE\n\nAn adviser in a mission is asked to provide a rapid course on forensic collection. Instead of immediately organizing trainers and materials, the adviser first investigates if the lack of forensic evidence is actually due to a lack of laboratory equipment or poor chain-of-custody procedures within the unit's organizational systems.",
      "pass": true
    },
